{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_36agokev5aisovxqmhtkcmyx5kiow4jphsbjilsljcd6ktegou7q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_0172aabmzRHFVF55wEiV1PTK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write state config tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are writing ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification project.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dNzXE1\n- `c_src/` = original C (MuJS 1.3.8). READ ONLY, never modify.\n- `translation/` = Rust cdylib translation. You may ONLY create/edit the ONE test file assigned to you: `translation/tests/state.rs`.\n- Do NOT modify translation/src/**, Cargo.toml, tests/common/mod.rs, SYMBOLS.md, ERRORS.md, CONFIGS.md, or anything in c_src/.\n\nFIRST read `translation/tests/common/mod.rs` fully — it is the harness. It exposes:\n- `libs()` -> &'static Libs with `.c` and `.r` (both `Api`), each field being a dlsym'd `unsafe extern \"C\" fn` for a MuJS symbol (names identical to the C symbols).\n- `diff(tag: &str, f: impl Fn(&Api))` — runs `f` once against the C library and once against the Rust library, each in a forked child with stdout+stderr captured, and asserts the captured bytes AND the exit status are identical. This is how every test must be written.\n- printing helpers `p_int`, `p_uint`, `p_num`, `p_str`, `p_line`, `p_ptr_nonnull`, `cs(\"literal\")` (CString), `newstate(api, flags)`, and `Rng` (deterministic xorshift; use a fixed seed).\n- Constants JS_STRICT, JS_READONLY, JS_DONTENUM, JS_DONTCONF, JS_REGEXP_*, REG_*.\n\nRULES for test bodies:\n- Never print raw pointer values, addresses, or current time — output must be deterministic and identical between the two libraries. Use `p_ptr_nonnull` for pointers.\n- Print all values you obtain, so divergences show up as byte differences.\n- Use only the harness helpers + libc for printing (do not use println!/format! inside a `diff` closure; outside closures it's fine).\n- Property-style: for numeric/string inputs use `Rng::new(<fixed seed>)` and loop over MANY (e.g. 200-2000) generated inputs inside a single `diff` closure, printing each result.\n- Free states you create with `(api.js_freestate)(J)` where the row is about normal operation.\n\nYOUR ASSIGNMENT — `translation/tests/state.rs`, covering these CONFIGS.md rows (read `translation/CONFIGS.md` rows 1-10 for details):\n1. js_newstate/js_freestate: default alloc, flags=0\n2. same with flags=JS_STRICT (also run a small strict-mode script through js_dostring to show the flag took effect)\n3. js_newstate with a CUSTOM js_Alloc + actx: write two `unsafe extern \"C\" fn` allocators (realloc-style: `fn(ctx, ptr, size) -> *mut c_void`, size 0 = free; mirror `js_defaultalloc` in c_src/src/jsstate.c) that count calls/bytes in a static counter reachable from the fn, print the counts at the end. Both libraries must produce the identical allocation trace.\n4. js_newstate with flags = 2, 0xFF, -1, i32::MAX (out-of-range flag bits)\n5. js_setcontext/js_getcontext round-trip with NULL and non-NULL\n6. js_setreport + js_report with a custom report callback (`unsafe extern \"C\" fn(JS, *const c_char)` that printf's the message), plus js_report with the default report, plus report of an error raised by a failing js_dostring\n7. js_atpanic: install a custom panic fn, check the returned previous pointer is non-null, then restore\n8. js_gc(J, 0) and js_gc(J, 1) on a fresh state and after allocating many objects/strings via js_dostring\n9. js_setlimit runlimit: a script that stays under the limit and one that exceeds it (use js_dostring so the error is reported, e.g. runlimit 2000000 vs an infinite/long loop). Keep runtime small.\n10. js_setlimit memlimit: memlimit hit and not hit; plus direct js_malloc/js_realloc/js_free/js_strdup under a memlimit (use js_ploadstring/js_pcall or js_dostring; for direct js_malloc a memlimit overrun throws — do that inside a `js_savetry`/`js_endtry`-free child, it is fine for the child to die as long as BOTH die the same way).\n\nWrite one `#[test] fn` per row named `cfg01_...` .. `cfg10_...` so the mapping to CONFIGS rows is obvious.\n\nVerify with: `cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dNzXE1/translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test state -- --test-threads=1 2>&1 | tail -40`\n(The C .so is already built at c_src/build/libmujs.so; the Rust .so at translation/target/release/libmujs.so.)\n\nIf a test reveals a DIVERGENCE between the C and Rust libraries, do NOT try to fix translation/src — that is someone else's job. Keep the test in place (failing) and report the exact divergence.\n\nFINAL REPORT (be concise): list each test fn, whether it passes, and for any failure the exact diverging output lines/status as printed by the harness."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2454,"cache_read_input_tokens":118716,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c","uuid":"f7eb2130-a329-43ca-bd65-6e2be38395c1","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:47:15.481Z"}
