ntically), n = 0/1/5\n- 63: js_construct / js_pconstruct on: a JS constructor function, a built-in constructor (`Object`, `Array`, `Error`, `RegExp`), and a non-constructor (identical failure required), n args 0/1/2\n- 79: js_savetry + js_endtry + js_throw. NOTE: `js_savetry` returns a `jmp_buf` that must be used with `setjmp`; you cannot call `setjmp` from Rust. Instead exercise the try machinery indirectly: (a) run scripts with try/catch/finally through js_dostring, (b) call js_savetry and js_endtry in a balanced pair without throwing and print js_gettop before/after, (c) exhaust the try stack by calling js_savetry 70 times (JS_TRYLIMIT is 64) and observe the identical failure in both libraries, (d) trigger js_throw from inside a C callback invoked via js_pcall so the library's own setjmp catches it (build the error with js_newerror + js_throw).\n- 80: js_savetrypc — same treatment: call it with a null pc in a balanced pair and print js_gettop; and exhaust the limit.\n- 81: js_trap(J, pc) with pc = 0 and a few small values on a fresh state and inside a running script (js_trap dumps the stack/environment to stdout — that output is exactly what must match). Read c_src/src/jsrun.c for what it prints; if it prints pointer addresses, note that in a comment and post-process by NOT using it in that mode (or verify empirically first — if addresses appear the test cannot be byte-identical; in that case restrict the test to states where output is address-free and say so in a comment).\n- 82: jsB_init on a bare `js_newstate` state plus each jsB_init* individually (js_newstate already calls jsB_init — check c_src/src/jsstate.c; if so, call the individual ones a second time on an initialised state and dump the resulting global names with js_pushiterator/js_nextiterator); also jsB_propf / jsB_propn / jsB_props on a pushed object, then enumerate + read back\n- 83: js_newarguments on a fresh state (dump the resulting object's properties), and js_newfunction / js_newscript with a 