rguments` as assignment targets). Identical errors are a pass.\n- row 66: `jsP_parsefunction(J, filename, params, body)` + `jsC_compilefunction(J, ast)` with: 0 params, 1 param, 5 params, params with whitespace, duplicate params, invalid params, empty body, body with a return, body with a syntax error; then instantiate with js_newfunction (see row 64 for how) and call it, printing the result.\n- row 76: `js_intern(J, s)` for many strings (short, long, empty, UTF-8, 300 random strings from a fixed-seed Rng, plus repeated interning of the same string) — print whether repeated interning returns the same pointer (compare pointers for EQUALITY only, never print addresses), then `jsS_dumpstrings(J)` (its stdout is what must match), then `jsS_freestrings(J)` and dump again.\n- row 77: `js_malloc` / `js_realloc` / `js_free` / `js_strdup` — allocate many blocks of assorted sizes (incl. 0 and 1), write and read back a pattern, realloc up and down, free; then the same with `js_setlimit(J, 0, memlimit)` set so the limit is (a) not reached and (b) exceeded (the overrun throws — identical behaviour in both libraries is a pass). Print only sizes/contents, never addresses.\n- row 78: `js_putc` / `js_puts` / `js_putm` into a `js_Buffer*` (the Api takes `*mut Buf`; start from a null `Buf` pointer as the C code does — read the C definition of js_Buffer and js_putc in c_src/src to see the exact idiom, e.g. `js_Buffer *sb = NULL; js_puts(J, &sb, \"x\");`). Grow the buffer across the doubling boundary (write > 512, > 1024, > 4096 bytes total in small pieces), mix putc/puts/putm, then print the accumulated buffer contents (the buffer struct's layout is in c_src/src/jsi.h — you can read the `s` field by treating the pointer as a byte pointer at the right offset; use `p_str` on the string once NUL-terminated as the C code does with js_putc(J,&sb,0)).\n\nWrite one `#[test] fn` per row: `cfg64_...`, `cfg65_...`, `cfg66_...`, `cfg67_...`, `cfg68_...`, `cfg76_...`, `cfg77_...`, `cfg78_...`.\n\nVerify with: `cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dNzXE1/translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test lexparse -- --test-threads=1 2>&1 | tail -60`\n\nIf a test reveals a DIVERGENCE between the C and Rust libraries, do NOT fix translation/src — keep the failing test and report the exact divergence.\n\nFINAL REPORT (concise): each test fn, pass/fail, and for failures the exact diverging lines/status.","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-8cjUpj/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-dNzXE1/5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c/tasks/a8e6f473dc42f342b.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
