irror how c_src/src/regexp.c uses it) that counts allocations in a static counter; print the allocation/free counts so the two libraries' allocation traces are compared as well. Include a case where the custom allocator returns NULL after N allocations, so the \"cannot allocate regular expression\" path is exercised — both libraries must fail identically.\n- row 53: `js_newregexp(J, pattern, flags)` for all 8 combinations of JS_REGEXP_G|I|M plus an out-of-range flag value (e.g. 255, -1) over ~15 patterns; then `js_isregexp`, `js_toregexp` (print non-null-ness), `js_tostring` of the regexp object, and read its `source`, `global`, `ignoreCase`, `multiline`, `lastIndex` properties with js_getproperty; also `js_newregexp` with an invalid pattern (must throw identically).\n- row 54: `js_RegExp_prototype_exec(J, re, text)`: get `re` from `js_toregexp` after `js_newregexp`, call it repeatedly on the same text with the global flag set (so `lastIndex` advances) and without it; print the resulting match object (index, input, and every capture) by reading properties off the pushed result, plus the regexp's `lastIndex` after each call, until no more matches. Also drive the same thing from JavaScript via `js_dostring` (`/re/g.exec(...)`, `String.replace`, `String.match`, `String.split` with a regexp) so the composed pipeline is covered.\n\nWrite one `#[test] fn` per row: `cfg53_...`, `cfg54_...`, `cfg55_...`, `cfg56_...`. Keep total runtime under ~60 seconds; if the random pattern loop is slow, reduce the subject set for random patterns (but keep at least 200 random patterns).\n\nVerify with: `cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dNzXE1/translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test regexp -- --test-threads=1 2>&1 | tail -60`\n\nIf a test reveals a DIVERGENCE between the C and Rust libraries, do NOT fix translation/src — keep the failing test and report the exact divergence (the exact pattern/subject/flags that diverge).\n\nFINAL REPORT (concise): each test fn, pass/fail, and for failures the exact diverging inputs and output lines.","uuid":"482e4c71-13ce-46e1-9228-30a7408121e8","session_id":"5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c"}
