als it installs so the corpus scripts run.\n\nYOUR ASSIGNMENT — `translation/tests/scripts.rs`, CONFIGS.md rows 57, 58, 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 94:\n- row 57: for EVERY file in `dtest/js/*.js` (there are ~100; read them at runtime with std::fs from `concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/../dtest/js\")`), run it through `js_dostring` on a NON-strict state that has the driver-like globals (`print`, etc.) installed, and compare C vs Rust output. Use ONE `#[test] fn` that loops over the files and calls `diff(file_name, ...)` per file so a failure names the file.\n- row 58: same corpus on a JS_STRICT state.\n- row 89: run the existing C drivers and compare their stdout byte-for-byte: build them if needed with\n  `gcc -O1 -I../c_src/include -o driver_c driver.c ../c_src/build/libmujs.so -lm -Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN/../c_src/build'` and the same for `driver_r` against `../translation/target/release/libmujs.so` (run from the `dtest` directory; the binaries `driver_c`/`driver_r` may already exist — rebuild them so they are fresh). Then, from the Rust test, run `driver_c` and `driver_r` with std::process::Command for the modes `-api`, `-lowlevel`, `-regexp`, `-ctx` and for a handful of corpus files with the extra args (`strict`, `dumpstrings`, `limit`, `memlimit`) and assert stdout+stderr+exit status match (normalise `0x[0-9a-f]+` hex addresses to `PTR` exactly like `dtest/run.sh` does). Also run `bash dtest/run.sh` as part of this test only if it completes within 300 seconds; otherwise skip it and say so in a comment.\n- row 90: Date built-ins with a FIXED timezone: set the TZ environment variable at the start of the test process (`std::env::set_var(\"TZ\", \"UTC\")` before the first library call, and also test with TZ=\"America/New_York\" and TZ=\"Asia/Kolkata\" — call the libc `tzset()` after changing it) and NEVER use the current time. Cover: `new Date(0)`, `new Date(ms)` for many fixed ms values incl. negatives and huge ones, `Date.UTC(...)`, `Date.parse` of many string format