{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_018joE3sP62u93PR37wnpSkQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MuJS C -> Rust translation conventions (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n2\t\n3\tWe are translating the MuJS C library in `c_src/` into the Rust cdylib in\n4\t`translation/`. The Rust library must be a *mechanical, behaviour identical*\n5\ttranslation: same algorithms, same order of checks, same output bytes, same\n6\tbugs. Do NOT improve, refactor, modernise or \"fix\" anything.\n7\t\n8\t## Ground rules\n9\t\n10\t* Work only on the file you were assigned. NEVER modify any other file in\n11\t  `translation/src/`, and NEVER modify anything in `c_src/`.\n12\t* The crate is one Rust crate; every C file becomes one Rust module.\n13\t* Everything is `unsafe` raw pointer code. Do not use `String`, `Vec`, `Box`,\n14\t  `format!`, `println!`, iterators, or any Rust std allocation. Use only the\n15\t  libc bindings and the helpers already declared in `src/jsi.rs`.\n16\t* Never print with Rust macros. Use the libc `printf`/`putchar`/`fputs`/\n17\t  `snprintf`/`sprintf`/`fprintf` bindings from `src/jsi.rs` so output bytes and\n18\t  buffering match the C library exactly.\n19\t* Integer arithmetic must wrap like C: the crate is built with\n20\t  `overflow-checks = false`, but where the C code clearly relies on wrapping or\n21\t  on shifts >= bit width, use `wrapping_*` methods.\n22\t\n23\t## Module skeleton\n24\t\n25\t```rust\n26\t//! Translation of <name>.c\n27\t\n28\tuse crate::*;            // types, constants, libc bindings, other modules\n29\t\n30\t/* ... translated code ... */\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\t`use crate::*;` gives you: everything from `src/jsi.rs` (all types, constants,\n34\tlibc `extern \"C\"` bindings, helper functions) plus every public function of\n35\tevery other module. Macros (`cs!`, `js_typeerror!`, `js_try!`, `vol!`, ...) are\n36\tavailable everywhere automatically (`#[macro_use] mod jsi`).\n37\t\n38\tIf you need a libc function that is not declared yet, add your own\n39\t`extern \"C\" { ... }` block at the top of your file (do not edit jsi.rs).\n40\t\n41\t## Naming / linkage\n42\t\n43\t* A C function that is NOT `static` becomes\n44\t  ```rust\n45\t  #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n46\t  pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn name(args) -> ret { ... }\n47\t  ```\n48\t  Its exported symbol name must match the C linker name exactly.\n49\t* A C `static` function becomes a private `unsafe fn name(...)`. Do not put\n50\t  `#[no_mangle]` or `pub` on it (private avoids name clashes between modules).\n51\t* The stub file you are replacing already contains the exact signatures of the\n52\t  non-static functions of your C file. Keep those signatures byte for byte.\n53\t* Functions used as `js_CFunction` callbacks must be\n54\t  `unsafe extern \"C\" fn(J: *mut js_State)` and are passed as `Some(name)`.\n55\t\n56\t## Type mapping\n57\t\n58\t| C | Rust |\n59\t|---|---|\n60\t| `int`, `unsigned`, `short`, `char`, `double` | `c_int`, `c_uint`, `c_short`, `c_char`, `f64` |\n61\t| `char *` / `const char *` | `*mut c_char` / `*const c_char` |\n62\t| `void *` | `*mut c_void` |\n63\t| `NULL` | `null()` / `null_mut()` |\n64\t| `js_State *J` | `*mut js_State` |\n65\t| struct field named `type` | field named `type_` |\n66\t| `enum js_Class` / `enum js_Type` field | `c_int` with the `JS_C*` / `JS_T*` constants |\n67\t\n68\tAll structs are declared in `src/jsi.rs` with `#[repr(C)]` and identical\n69\tlayout. Read that file before starting.\n70\t\n71\t### js_Value\n72\t\n73\t`js_Value` is a `#[repr(C)]` union. Use the helpers from jsi.rs:\n74\t\n75\t```rust\n76\tvtype(v)                  /* v->t.type as c_int */\n77\tsetvtype(v, JS_TNUMBER)   /* v->t.type = JS_TNUMBER */\n78\t(*v).u.number             /* v->u.number */\n79\t(*v).u.boolean, (*v).u.litstr, (*v).u.memstr, (*v).u.object\n80\tshrstrp(v)                /* char* to v->u.shrstr */\n81\tstrp(memstr)              /* char* to js_String->p */\n82\tjsv_isstring(v)           /* JSV_ISSTRING(v) */\n83\tjsv_tostring_raw(v)       /* JSV_TOSTRING(v) */\n84\tOFF_VALUE_TYPE            /* soffsetof(js_Value, t.type) == 15 */\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\tFlexible array members (`js_Property.name`, `js_Iterator.name`,\n88\t`js_String.p`, `js_StringNode.string`) are `[c_char; 1]` fields; get a pointer\n89\twith `propname(p)`, `itername(p)`, `strp(s)`, `nodestring(n)`, and allocate\n90\twith the `OFF_*` offset constants (e.g. `OFF_PROPERTY_NAME + n`).\n91\t\n92\t## String literals\n93\t\n94\t`cs!(\"hello\")` produces a NUL terminated `*const c_char`. Use it for every C\n95\tstring literal, including format strings.\n96\t\n97\t## Errors and exceptions\n98\t\n99\tThe `js_*error` functions are variadic in C. Use these macros, which format\n100\tinto a 256 byte buffer with libc `snprintf` exactly like the C originals and\n101\tthen throw:\n102\t\n103\t```rust\n104\tjs_error!(J, \"stack underflow!\");\n105\tjs_typeerror!(J, \"'%s' is read-only\", name);\n106\tjs_rangeerror!(J, \"invalid array length\");\n107\tjs_referenceerror!(J, \"'%s' is not defined\", str);\n108\tjs_syntaxerror!(J, \"...\", ...);\n109\tjs_evalerror!(J, \"...\");\n110\tjs_urierror!(J, \"...\");\n111\t```\n112\t\n113\tThey diverge (`-> !`), so no `return` is needed after them. `js_throw(J)` also\n114\tdiverges.\n115\t\n116\tIf your C file has its own `static` variadic error helper (for example\n117\t`jsY_error` in jslex.c), translate it as a non-variadic\n118\t`unsafe fn jsY_error_str(J: *mut js_State, msg: *const c_char) -> !` plus a\n119\tlocal macro that formats with `snprintf` into a buffer **of exactly the same\n120\tsize as the C original** and calls it:\n121\t\n122\t```rust\n123\tmacro_rules! jsY_error {\n124\t    ($J:expr, $fmt:expr $(, $a:expr)*) => {{\n125\t        let mut __b: [c_char; 512] = [0; 512];\n126\t        snprintf(__b.as_mut_ptr(), 512, cs!($fmt) $(, $a)*);\n127\t        jsY_error_str($J, __b.as_ptr())\n128\t    }};\n129\t}\n130\t```\n131\t\n132\t`jsC_error(J, node, fmt, ...)` already exists in `src/varargs.rs`; from Rust\n133\tcode call `crate::varargs::jsC_error_str(J, node, msg)` through a local macro\n134\tin the same style (buffer size 256, as in the C `msgbuf`).\n135\t\n136\t## try/catch (setjmp/longjmp)\n137\t\n138\t```rust\n139\tif js_try!(J) != 0 {\n140\t    /* exception path */\n141\t    js_throw(J);\n142\t}\n143\t/* body */\n144\tjs_endtry(J);\n145\t```\n146\t\n147\t`js_try!` MUST be written directly in the function whose frame the exception\n148\treturns to (never inside a helper function or closure).\n149\t\n150\t**Important:** any local variable that is assigned inside the `try` body and\n151\tread in the exception path must be accessed with the volatile helpers, exactly\n152\twhere the C source used the `volatile` qualifier:\n153\t\n154\t```rust\n155\tlet mut out: *mut c_char = null_mut();\n156\tif js_try!(J) != 0 {\n157\t    js_free(J, vol!(out) as *mut c_void);\n158\t    js_throw(J);\n159\t}\n160\tsetvol!(out, js_malloc(J, n) as *mut c_char);\n161\t... use vol!(out) ...\n162\tjs_endtry(J);\n163\tjs_free(J, vol!(out) as *mut c_void);\n164\t```\n165\t\n166\t## Control flow translation\n167\t\n168\t* `goto label;` inside a function: use a labelled block\n169\t  ```rust\n170\t  'body: { ... break 'body; ... }   /* code after the block == label target */\n171\t  ```\n172\t  or a boolean flag plus restructuring. Keep the semantics identical.\n173\t* C `switch` with fallthrough: replicate the fallthrough explicitly.\n174\t* `for (a; b; c) { ... continue; }` -> `while` loop, but remember `continue`\n175\t  must still execute the increment `c`.\n176\t* `while (x--)`, `while (--x)` etc: translate the pre/post semantics carefully.\n177\t* Do not use `match` on constants unless every arm is a `pub const` (they are).\n178\t\n179\t## Static (file scope) variables\n180\t\n181\t```rust\n182\tstatic mut FOO: c_int = 0;              /* mutable C static */\n183\tstatic TABLE: [c_int; 3] = [1, 2, 3];   /* const C static */\n184\t```\n185\tFor arrays of string pointers use `[&str; N]` with `\"...\\0\"` entries and take\n186\t`.as_ptr() as *const c_char` (raw pointers are not `Sync` so they cannot be in\n187\ta plain `static`). Self-referential statics (like the AA-tree sentinels) are\n188\tinitialised lazily; see `src/jsproperty.rs` for the pattern.\n189\t\n190\t## Examples already finished (read them for style)\n191\t\n192\t`src/jsvalue.rs`, `src/jsrun.rs`, `src/jsproperty.rs`, `src/jsgc.rs`,\n193\t`src/jsintern.rs`, `src/jsstate.rs`, `src/jserror.rs`, `src/utf.rs`.\n194\t\n195\t## Verifying\n196\t\n197\tFrom `translation/`:\n198\t\n199\t```\n200\tcargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -A8 \"src/<yourfile>.rs\"\n201\t```\n202\t\n203\tOther stub modules are still unimplemented, so ignore errors that are not in\n204\tyour own file. Keep iterating until your file has no errors and no warnings\n205\tother than dead-code ones. Do not stop with a partially translated file: every\n206\tfunction of your C file must be present.\n207\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01E8ecAiGGHYTpr8UWXCrPR2","session_id":"5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c","uuid":"0f79ca3c-1f3b-4a4d-8a86-5b842a0ad7f8","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:52:25.735Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write script corpus + builtin tests"}
