to the Api — set `CUR = api as *const Api;` at the top of each closure; see `dtest/driver.c` for the C version of such a print function).\n- For errors reachable only through the C API: call the C API function directly with the invalid input and print what comes back (return code, or, for functions that throw, let the child die — the harness compares exit status, so identical aborts/exits pass).\n- For rows 57 and 58 (`assert` in `minus`, jsdtoa.c): the C library is built WITH assertions, so a failing assert aborts with SIGABRT. Call `js_grisu2` with an input that hits it (`0.0` is known to abort in C). Both libraries must abort the same way; do not work around it.\n- If a row is genuinely unreachable from outside the library (e.g. an out-of-memory-only path), write the test for the CLOSEST reachable trigger and add a comment `// row N: only reachable under allocation failure; nearest reachable trigger is ...`. Never delete a row's test.\n\nVerify with: `cd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-dNzXE1/translation && timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test errors_1 -- --test-threads=1 2>&1 | tail -60`\n\nEvery test must actually EXERCISE its branch — sanity check by confirming the printed error message matches the message in the ERRORS.md row (if it does not, your trigger reaches a different check; fix the trigger). Print the row number in the output of each test so it is traceable.\n\nIf a test reveals a DIVERGENCE between C and Rust, do NOT fix translation/src — keep the failing test and report the exact divergence.\n\nFINAL REPORT (concise): a list `row N: pass|fail|unreachable-proxy` for all 64 rows, then the exact diverging output for any failures.","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-8cjUpj/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-dNzXE1/5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c/tasks/a9966d23261dd6f73.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
