k — print whether the returned state pointer is null (`p_ptr_nonnull`).\n- Rows 234-235: `js_regexec` returning REG_NOMATCH (1) and the recursion limit during matching (a catastrophic-backtracking subject like `a{25}` against `(a+)+b`); print return codes.\n- Rows 236-241: array length limit (`new Array(-1)`, `a.length = -1`, `a.length = 1e10`), string length limit (repeated concatenation is too slow — use `Array(n).join(str)` or `String.prototype.repeat`-style growth only if it stays under a few hundred MB; if it cannot be reached in reasonable time/memory, use the closest reachable trigger and say so in a comment), environment/value stack overflow (deep recursion; pushing >4096 values via the C API), run limit and memory limit via `js_setlimit`.\n- Row 242 (OUT-OF-RANGE ENUM VALUES — important): `js_newstate(NULL, NULL, 0x7fffffff)` and other bogus flag ints; `js_newregexp(J, \"a\", 255)` and `-1`; `js_regexec(prog, s, sub, 255)`; `jsV_newobject(J, 99, NULL)` and `-1`; `jsY_tokenstring(9999)`, `(-1)`, `i32::MIN`; `js_toprimitive(J, idx, 99)`; `js_strtol(s, &ep, 99)` and radix `-1`; `js_type` / `js_typeof` on out-of-range stack indices; `js_defproperty` with atts `255`; `js_pushiterator(J, idx, 99)`. Print every result. C accepts any int here, so the Rust must match bit-for-bit.\n- Row 243 (NULL / boundary pointers): `js_pushstring(J, \"\")`, `js_pushlstring(J, s, 0)` and with `n` longer than the string, `js_intern(J, \"\")`, `js_regcomp(\"\", 0, &err)`, `js_regexec(prog, \"\", NULL, 0)`, `js_utflen(\"\")`, `jsU_chartorune` on a truncated sequence, `js_getproperty(J, idx, \"\")`, `js_strtod(\"\", &ep)`, `js_grisu2` on 0.0 (this one aborts in C — identical abort is a pass), and stack index 0 / out-of-range indices (e.g. `js_tostring(J, 999)`, `js_tostring(J, -999)`).\n\nSanity check: the printed message must match the message in the ERRORS.md row; if not, your trigger hit a different check — fix it. Print the row number in each test's output.\n\nVerify with: `cd /lo