ia the harness's public `capture`) to confirm the two sides agree on real content, not on emptiness:\n\n- **Allocator trace (row 3)** byte-identical, including `ev00 new size=48552` (`sizeof(js_State)`) and `ev01 new size=65536` (`JS_STACKSIZE * sizeof(js_Value)`); after `js_newstate`: `n_new=1033, bytes_requested=194775, seq_hash=e615643e70e17320`; after `js_freestate`: `live=0, n_free=1033, n_freenull=1`. The full-order-sensitive `seq_hash` matches, so the Rust translation reproduces the C allocation *sequence*, not merely the totals.\n- **GC reports (row 8)** identical strings, e.g. `garbage collected (46%): 0/1 envs, 1/1 funs, 302/640 objs, 600/1296 props, 0/0 strs`.\n- **Panic paths (row 7b/c/d, row 10d/e/f)** both children print the same line and die with `signal 6` (`abort()` after `js_throw` with `trytop==0`).\n- **Limits** produce `script ran too long` / `out of memory` through the report hook with matching `rc`/`top`.\n- Sweep ranges were tuned to straddle the pass/fail boundary rather than sitting on one side: runlimit boundary for the fixed script is 500→600 (sweep draws 1..900), memlimit boundary is 8000→10000 (sweep draws 1..20000). So the 200-iteration property loops in rows 9 and 10 genuinely compare *where* each library trips its limit, i.e. exact VM instruction counting and per-allocation memlimit accounting.\n\n## Notes / behaviours pinned by these tests\n\n- `js_setlimit` only checks `> 0`, so negative and `i32::MAX` limits mean \"no limit\" — covered explicitly.\n- After a runlimit hit, `J->runlimit` stays at 1 (`jsrun.c:1602`), so every later script fails until `js_setlimit(J,0,0)`; asserted.\n- `js_setreport(J, NULL)` silences `js_report`; the default `js_defaultreport` is unrecoverable once replaced, so it is exercised first on a fresh state.\n- `js_atpanic(J, NULL)` makes `js_throw` fall straight to `abort()`; covered as row 7d.\n- Row 4 uses 150 random 32-bit flag words plus `{0,1,2,3,4,0xFF,-1,i32::MIN,i32::MAX}`; only bit 0 is observable,