## The divergence (kept failing, translation/src untouched)\n`js_grisu2(+0.0, buf, &K)` — and identically `-0.0` — trips `assert(x.f >= y.f)` in `minus()` (`c_src/src/jsdtoa.c:387`). Both libraries die with **SIGABRT (signal 6)**, but the stderr bytes differ:\n\n```\nDIVERGENCE in `cfg20_grisu2_zero`\n  C status: signal 6\n  R status: signal 6\n  line 1:\n    C: numbers-2c66c1a346f353d7: .../c_src/src/jsdtoa.c:387: minus: Assertion `x.f >= y.f' failed.\n    R: <missing>          (Rust child produced 0 bytes)\n```\n\nCause: `translation/src/jsdtoa.rs:262-271` translates both asserts as bare `if !cond { abort(); }` (the C `assert` text is left as a comment), so the Rust child aborts silently while glibc's `assert` prints a diagnostic. Same signal, same (empty) stdout, only the assert message differs — i.e. a diagnostics-only divergence, not a numeric one. Note the C message embeds the local source path and test-binary name, so it is not reproducible by any translation.\n\n## Two other behaviours worth knowing (both match, so tests pass)\n- `js_strtol(s, ep, 99)`: with radix ≥ 81 the C loop accepts the NUL terminator (`table['\\0'] == 80 < 99`) and walks off the end of the string until it faults. Both libraries **segfault (signal 11) with zero output**, so this configuration only verifies identical crash behaviour; it is isolated in `cfg19_strtol_radix99` so it cannot swallow the other radices' output. Radix 37 and -1 terminate normally.\n- `js_grisu2` on inf/NaN does *not* assert; it returns garbage-but-deterministic digits (e.g. inf → n=16, K=293, `1797693134862316`), and both sides agree, including for the 2000 raw-bit-pattern doubles.\n- Crashing children were each costing ~9 s of `systemd-coredump`; the test file sets `RLIMIT_CORE=0` for its own process tree (`no_core_dumps()`), which does not affect compared stdout/stderr or exit status."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3179,"ca