{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a8e6f473dc42f342b","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HGU77t6bQqvV6uY51qcQTc","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-8cjUpj/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-dNzXE1/5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c/tasks/a8e6f473dc42f342b.output","summary":"Done. `translation/tests/lexparse.rs` (1063 lines, 8 tests) — **all 8 pass, no divergences found.**\n\n## Result\n\n| test fn | cases (each its own forked C/Rust pair) | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cfg64_parse_compilescript_nonstrict` | 52 scripts, `jsP_parse` + `jsC_compilescript(ast,0)` + `jsP_freeparse` + `js_newscript` + `js_pcall` | pass |\n| `cfg65_parse_compilescript_strict` | same 52 with `default_strict=1`, plus 14 strict-only-invalid sources run both lax and strict (80 cases) | pass |\n| `cfg66_parsefunction_compilefunction` | 26 (params, body) combos, instantiated with `js_newfunction` over a `jsR_newenvironment(global,NULL)` and called with 0/1/5 args | pass |\n| `cfg67_lex_tokens` | 55 sources through `jsY_initlex`/`jsY_lex` | pass |\n| `cfg68_lexjson_tokens` | 31 sources through `jsY_lexjson` | pass |\n| `cfg76_intern_dumpstrings` | 3 cases: 23 fixed + 300 seeded random strings, pointer-identity checks, `jsS_dumpstrings`, empty-table free/dump, compiler-interned table | pass |\n| `cfg77_malloc_realloc_free_strdup` | 7 cases: 14 sizes written/checksummed/realloc'd up+down, `js_strdup`, size-0 throw, memlimit not-reached / exceeded (malloc, realloc, strdup) | pass |\n| `cfg78_putc_puts_putm` | 5 cases: growth past 64/128/512/1024/4096, mixed putc/puts/putm, raw bytes, memlimit overrun | pass |\n\n## Design notes worth knowing\n\n- **Uncaught throws**: each case installs a `js_atpanic` handler that prints `PANIC=<js_trystring(-1)>` and `_exit(9)`, so the *error message and the exit status* are part of the compared bytes (instead of `abort()` + core dumps). This is what makes the ~90 throwing cases meaningful rather than just \"both crashed\". Verified messages match, e.g. `SyntaxError: lex.js:5: unexpected character: '@'` (line counting across LF/CR/CRLF/U+2028/U+2029), `number with leading zero`, `duplicated flag in regular expression`, `out of memory`, `EvalError: script.js:1: invalid use of 'eval'` vs the strict-mode `'eval' is read-only in strict mode`.\n- One case per `diff` call, because an uncaught throw ends the child; this keeps a throwing input from masking later inputs.\n- `print` is defined by mujs' `main.c`, not the library, so `compile_and_run`/`compile_function` install their own `print` cfunction that echoes every argument — that is what makes the *compiled byte code's* side effects and their ordering observable, not just the completion value.\n- Regexp/divide disambiguation needs no flag: `jsY_lex` decides from `J->lasttoken` (`isregexpcontext`), so the wart is driven by token sequences (`{} /re/` → `/`,ident,`/` vs `typeof /t/i` → TK_REGEXP), verified in the token dumps.\n- `jsS_freestrings` does not clear `J->strings`, so \"dump again after free\" is only exercised on an *empty* table (and the populated state is abandoned without `js_freestate`); dumping a freed AA-tree would be a use-after-free whose output is heap-layout dependent, i.e. a false-positive divergence generator.\n- `js_malloc(J,0)` / `js_realloc(p,0)` throw (`js_defaultalloc` returns NULL → `js_outofmemory`), so those are isolated cases; realloc-down never goes to 0.\n- Buffer inspection reads `js_Buffer.n`/`.m` at offsets 0/4 and `s` at offset 8 (`struct js_Buffer { int n, m; char s[64]; }`), printing sizes, a rolling checksum and the NUL-terminated contents — never addresses (`p_ptr_nonnull` only).","usage":{"total_tokens":148791,"tool_uses":50,"duration_ms":768302},"uuid":"fc22f679-7093-4d00-a1a8-32a2be7241f0","session_id":"5c8219bc-b0e9-40a9-995a-ed544487508c"}
